  + Day 178 (#749 item 1) gave that whole boundary its **second door: a persisted per-directory trust store**, because `--trust-project` applied to one run and a capability reachable only by retyping a flag is the shape that trains people to alias it away — i.e. back to the unsafe default, by habit (#749 says so out loud, and Claude Code / Cursor / VS Code all ask once per folder and remember). `set_trust_project()` is still called from exactly **one** site in `parse_args`, which now has **three** sources: `--trust-project` (unchanged, one run, byte-identical), the new `--trust-project-always` (this run **and** `config_paths::remember_trusted_dir(cwd)`), or — with neither flag — `config_paths::dir_is_trusted(cwd)`. It is the same two-source-OR shape as `continue_on_silence` (#794) and `wait_for_reset` (Day 178), and the flag is in `KNOWN_FLAGS` taking no value (so deliberately *not* in the value-taking list `check_flag_values` scans). **A stored decision grants the full boundary** — project MCP servers, `[permissions].allow`, shell hooks (#820) and `.yoyo/goal_verify.md` (#761) all consult `is_trust_project()`, so recording a directory turns all four on there for every later session. **Both events are announced, because a silent trust grant is a bug even when it is the right grant** (the `⚡ auto-continuing` rule): the pure `trust_remembered_message` names the directory recorded, the store file's full path and that removing the line revokes it, and the pure `trusted_by_store_message` — the important one — says a run is live because of a `--trust-project-always` typed weeks ago and names the file, so a user can never discover that state by accident; both are silent under `is_quiet()` and glyph-free under `is_plain_output()`. **The default is unchanged and pinned:** neither flag and no store file → the trust path is unreachable and output is byte-identical. There is deliberately **no un-trust flag** — revoking is deleting a line from a plain-text file, and both messages name its path, so a fourth switch would be ceremony. **Deliberately not covered (follow-up #749 item 2): there is still no interactive "do you trust this folder?" prompt**, so trust is only ever granted by a flag the user types — which is also why a non-interactive run always takes the safe answer.
