  + **Known debt:** this pushed `src/setup.rs` to 2067 lines and onto `GRANDFATHERED_OVERSIZED_MODULES`; the split is blocked on ~20 private helpers its ~1200 lines of tests reach through `use super::*`, and the register entry says so. Day 178 (#749 item 1) added a **second persisted-user-level-state family** to this file, on the grounds that it is the same question one surface over — *which persisted user-level file governs this directory?* — and so belongs beside the precedence ladder rather than in a new module: the **workspace trust store** (`trusted_dirs_contains`, `append_trusted_dir`, `trusted_dirs_path`, `dir_is_trusted`, `remember_trusted_dir`), a plain-text file of one absolute directory path per line at `$XDG_CONFIG_HOME/yoyo/trusted_dirs` (resolved off the ladder's own XDG rung via `cli::user_config_path()`, deliberately **not** a second XDG resolver). The decision half is pure and table-tested — `trusted_dirs_contains(content, dir)` (a non-blank, non-`#` line that trims to exactly the dir's string) and `append_trusted_dir(content, dir)` (**idempotent**: already-present → `content` unchanged, never a duplicate line; exactly one trailing newline) — with all I/O in the three siblings at the call site. **Three rules that are the security design, each pinned by a test:** (1) the store is **user-level, never project-level** — nothing looks for a `./.yoyo/trusted_dirs`, because a repo that could trust itself defeats the whole boundary, the same provenance question `config_path_is_project_local` answers for configs; (2) **exact directory match only, no prefix or subdirectory inheritance** — trusting `/home/me/proj` trusts neither `/home/me/proj/vendor/evil` nor `/home/me/proj-other`, since a prefix match would silently widen a security control across directories the user never saw (the near-miss is pinned in both directions); (3) paths are stored **canonicalized**, so `.`/`..`/a symlinked checkout cannot present as a different string than the one that was trusted, and when canonicalization fails the helpers **refuse** rather than falling back to the raw path — "could not check" must not read as "trusted". A missing or unreadable store is `false`, never an error. The consumer is the single `set_trust_project()` site in `cli.rs` (see that bullet for the announcements and for what a stored decision actually grants).
